@* The public features page: the app's shop window, one scrolling story of what a seeker gets on
   the phone, ending in the store badges. Open to everyone, signed in or not, exactly like the
   privacy policy — the two public pages share PublicLayout.

   The "screenshots" are drawn in HTML inside CSS phone frames rather than shipped as PNGs: they
   follow the theme toggle and the chosen language the way the real app does, and they weigh
   nothing. Each is a still of a real screen (home, Panchangam, calendar, japa, puja, prayers) laid
   out from the same tokens the phone shell uses, with the real screens' labels pulled from the same
   dictionary keys. When real captures exist, a frame's .lp-screen is the thing to replace with an
   <img>; the frame, the glow and the motion around it stay.

   Motion: the hero animates on load with plain keyframes; everything below reveals as it scrolls
   into view ([data-reveal] + landing.js), the sky drifts with the scroll (--land-y), and browsers
   with scroll-driven animations get the phones settling into place as they enter. All of it is
   transform/opacity only, and all of it stands down under prefers-reduced-motion. A reveal that
   never gets its JS still appears (the land-safety keyframe), so the page is never blank.

   The store links and QR codes are placeholders until the listings exist: PlayStoreUrl follows
   from the fixed ApplicationId, AppStoreUrl is a dummy id, and the two codes live in
   wwwroot/landing/. Swap the two constants and regenerate the two SVGs when the apps go live. *@
@page "/features"
@page "/download"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@layout PublicLayout
@inherits LocalizedComponentBase
@inject IJSRuntime JS

